  "content": "To solve the expression $(a \\cdot b + c^3) - (a - b) \\cdot c$ with the given values $a = 137$, $b = 29$, and $c = 11$, we will break down the calculation into logical steps, performing the integer arithmetic explicitly for each component.\n\n### Step 1: Compute $a \\cdot b$\n$$\na \\cdot b = 137 \\cdot 29\n$$\nWe can decompose this multiplication:\n$$\n137 \\cdot 29 = 137 \\cdot (30 - 1) = (137 \\cdot 30) - (137 \\cdot 1)\n$$\n$$\n137 \\cdot 30 = 4110\n$$\n$$\n4110 - 137 = 3973\n$$\nSo, **$a \\cdot b = 3973$**.\n\n### Step 2: Compute $c^3$\n$$\nc^3 = 11^3 = 11 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 11\n$$\nFirst, compute $11^2$:\n$$\n11 \\cdot 11 = 121\n$$\nThen, multiply by 11:\n$$\n121 \\cdot 11 = 121 \\cdot (10 + 1) = 1210 + 121 = 1331\n$$\nSo, **$c^3 = 1331$**.\n\n### Step 3: Compute the first part: $(a \\cdot b + c^3)$\nNow we add the results from Step 1 and Step 2:\n$$\n3973 + 1331\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  3973 \\\\\n+ 1331 \\\\\n\\hline\n  5304\n\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, **$(a \\cdot b + c^3) = 5304$**.\n\n### Step 4: Compute $(a - b)$\n$$\na - b = 137 - 29\n$$\n$$\n137 - 29 = 108",
